<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The app's privacy manifest.

  Three honest claims and one required-reason declaration:

  * NSPrivacyTracking false and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains empty — the app makes NO network
    requests at all. That is not a policy, it is structural: a SwimZHKit source lint fails
    the build if URLSession, Network, NWConnection, CFSocket or CFStream appears in either
    target. Everything it answers comes from the SQLite store in this bundle.
  * NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes empty — nothing leaves the device, because nothing can.
  * NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes declares NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults with reason
    CA92.1. @AppStorage IS UserDefaults, S3a's local-only favourites use it, and shipping
    without the declaration is an ITMS-91055 upload rejection — a failure that only appears
    at upload time, which is exactly why it is done now rather than at submission.

  Reason code CA92.1, verbatim from Apple: "Declare this reason to access user defaults to
  read and write information that is only accessible to the app itself. This reason does not
  permit reading information that was written by other apps or the system, or writing
  information that can be accessed by other apps." That is precisely this app's use: its own
  defaults, in its own container. The other three User Defaults codes are all wrong here, and
  each for its own reason: 1C8F.1 is for defaults shared with an App Group, C56D.1 may only
  be declared by a third-party SDK wrapping UserDefaults, and AC6B.1 is MDM managed
  configuration. This app has no App Group, is not an SDK, and is not managed.

  Source, and why it is this one: Apple's RENDERED documentation page is client-side and
  returns no body content to an automated read — it defeated two attempts and is exactly how
  fabricated category names get into manifests. The codes above are taken from Apple's own
  machine-readable payload behind that page,
  https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/data/documentation/BundleResources/App-Privacy-Configuration/NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes/NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons.json
  retrieved 2026-08-24. A human should still eyeball CA92.1 in a browser before the first
  upload, but this is documentation-verified, not merely corroborated.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
